<h2>Can a wearable measure stress?</h2>
<p><strong>A wearable records signals that can inform a stress estimate; it does not directly measure your psychological stress or cortisol concentration.</strong> Heart rate, HRV and sleep describe different aspects of your body. Apps combine some of these records with their own assumptions.</p>
<h2>Start with the underlying records</h2>
<table><thead><tr><th scope="col">Record</th><th scope="col">Useful question</th><th scope="col">What it cannot establish</th></tr></thead><tbody>
<tr><td>HRV</td><td>How does a reading compare with my history using the same measurement method?</td><td>The amount of cortisol or the cause of a change</td></tr>
<tr><td>Heart rate</td><td>Was I resting, moving or exercising when this was recorded?</td><td>Whether psychological stress caused a faster pulse</td></tr>
<tr><td>Sleep</td><td>How much sleep was recorded and is the record complete?</td><td>Why I woke up or whether a hormone disorder is present</td></tr>
<tr><td>Wrist temperature</td><td>Does my device support it, and what is the recorded change?</td><td>A direct cortisol or core-temperature reading</td></tr>
</tbody></table>
<p>For Apple Watch, see Apple's descriptions of <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/120277">heart measurements</a>, <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/108906">sleep</a> and <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/102674">wrist temperature</a>. Availability varies with device, settings and wear time.</p>
<h2>Why two stress apps can disagree</h2>
<p>Apps can use different records, time windows, baselines and formulas. An HRV statistic such as SDNN is also different from RMSSD; compare like with like. A score of 60 in one app is not a standardized equivalent of 60 in another.</p>
<p>Check the latest measurement time, missing inputs and explanation behind the number. More frequent app updates do not necessarily mean a sensor took a new reading. Apple Watch background sampling is intermittent.</p>
<h2>How Cortisol+ uses the data</h2>
<p>Cortisol+ combines available HRV, sleep, heart rate and activity-related factors into a 0–100 wellness score. Its <a href="/how-it-works/">published methodology</a> explains the weights, baseline process and missing-data limitations. The score does not model an established cortisol concentration or clinically validated hormone curve.</p>
<p>Use it to explore recorded patterns and ask better questions about your routine. Do not use it to diagnose burnout, overtraining or a hormone disorder, or to verify that a supplement worked.</p>
<h2>A practical way to begin</h2>
<ol><li>Find the original readings and their timestamps in your health app.</li><li>Check which permissions and measurements your chosen stress app uses.</li><li>Review comparable readings over time alongside brief notes about your routine.</li><li>Keep symptoms and clinical decisions separate from a wearable score.</li></ol>
<p>Our <a href="/stress/apple-watch/">Apple Watch stress guide</a>, <a href="/compare/apple-watch-stress-apps/">app comparison</a> and <a href="/resources/stress-sleep-journal/">free observation journal</a> help with those steps.</p>
